  "query_preview": "..\nA pocket of more stable air and light winds between Tropical Storm\nSaudel and a secondary monsoon trough has moved over the Marianas.\nThis will allow for potentially heavy island effect showers and\nisolated thunderstorms to develop over Guam, Tinian, and Saipan, with\nthe highest rainfall over Guam. This will be additionally fueled by\nevapotranspiration from already saturated soils. The only \npotentially limiting factor would be if upper level cirrus bands from\nSaudel drift over the islands. By Friday night, winds will begin to \npick up for all islands with scattered showers and isolated \nthunderstorms moving into Tinian and Saipan as Saudel passes just \nnortheast. The monsoon tail of Saudel will bring moisture to all \nislands and any thunderstorms will have the potential for gusty winds\nand heavy rainfall. However, tropical storm conditions are not \nexpected at this time. This monsoon tail has the potential to sit \nover the Marianas for much of the rest of the forecast period but \nthis remains uncertain at this time. Residents of the Marianas should\ncontinue to monitor the forecast.",
